Why This Is Asked
- Demonstrates modeling hierarchical command structures with a single interface.
- Shows uniform treatment of leaves and composites, enabling recursive execution.
- Tests understanding of registration and delegation without touching client logic.

Key Concepts
- Composite pattern
- Uniform interface for leaves/composites
- Recursive command resolution
- Lightweight plugin-friendly design

Code Example
```javascript
class Command {
  execute(args) { throw new Error('not implemented'); }
  help() { throw new Error('not implemented'); }
}
class LeafCommand extends Command {
  constructor(name, action){ super(); this.name = name; this.action = action; }
  execute(args){ this.action(args); }
  help(){ return this.name; }
}
class CommandGroup extends Command {
  constructor(name){ super(); this.name = name; this.children = []; }
  add(cmd){ this.children.push(cmd); }
  execute(args){ const [sub] = args; const found = this.children.find(c => c.help() === sub);
    if(found) found.execute(args.slice(1));
  }
  help(){ return this.name + ' (' + this.children.map(c => c.help()).join(', ') + ')'; }
}

// Usage
const root = new CommandGroup('root');
const status = new LeafCommand('status', () => console.log('ok'));
const repo = new CommandGroup('repo');
repo.add(status);
root.add(repo);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Introduces a practical runtime plugin scenario: logging backends and formatters can be swapped without redeploying, exercising registry and strategy patterns in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern
- Strategy/Adapter via pluggable backends and formatters
- Open/Closed principle and runtime configurability

## Code Example

```typescript
interface LogBackend {
  log(level: string, message: string, meta?: any): void;
}
interface Formatter {
  format(level: string, message: string, meta?: any): string;
}
class Logger {
  private backends = new Map<string, LogBackend>();
  private formatter: Formatter;
  constructor(formatter: Formatter) { this.formatter = formatter; }
  register(name: string, backend: LogBackend) { this.backends.set(name, backend); }
  unregister(name: string) { this.backends.delete(name); }
  setFormatter(f: Formatter) { this.formatter = f; }
  log(level: string, message: string, meta?: any) {
    const formatted = this.formatter.format(level, message, meta);
    for (const b of this.backends.values()) b.log(level, formatted, meta);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle backpressure when multiple backends write synchronously?
- How would you ensure log order and handle failures in a backend without losing logs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design pluggable runtime extensions, handle concurrency considerations, and understand trade-offs between in-process versus out-of-process sinks.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin registry and dynamic loading at runtime
- Minimal interface contracts for telemetry sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and failure isolation
- Stateless versus stateful sinks and lifecycle management

## Code Example

```javascript
interface TelemetrySink {
  send(event: TelemetryEvent): Promise<void>;
  flush?(): Promise<void>;
  shutdown?(): Promise<void>;
}

class PluginRegistry {
  #sinks = new Map();

  register(name, factory) {
    this.#sinks.set(name, factory(this));
  }

  get(name) {
    return this.#sinks.get(name);
  }

  list() {
    return Array.from(this.#sinks.keys());
  }
}

class SyslogSink {
  #buffer = [];
  #batchSize = 100;
  #flushInterval = 5000;
  #paused = false;
  #controller = new AbortController();

  constructor(options = {}) {
    this.#batchSize = options.batchSize ?? 100;
    this.#flushInterval = options.flushInterval ?? 5000;
    this.#startFlushTimer();
  }

  async send(event) {
    if (this.#paused) {
      throw new Error('Backpressure: sink is paused');
    }

    this.#buffer.push(event);

    if (this.#buffer.length >= this.#batchSize) {
      await this.flush();
    }

    if (this.#buffer.length > this.#batchSize * 0.8) {
      this.#paused = true;
    }
  }

  async flush() {
    if (this.#buffer.length === 0) return;

    const batch = this.#buffer.splice(0, this.#batchSize);
    try {
      await this.#sendToSyslog(batch);
      this.#paused = false;
    } catch (err) {
      this.#buffer.unshift(...batch);
      throw err;
    }
  }

  async shutdown() {
    this.#controller.abort();
    await this.flush();
  }

  #startFlushTimer() {
    setInterval(() => this.flush(), this.#flushInterval);
  }

  async #sendToSyslog(batch) {
    // Send batch to syslog endpoint
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PluginRegistry();
registry.register('syslog', () => new SyslogSink({ batchSize: 50 }));
```

